  "passage_locator": {
    "label": "LI. THE HASTY WORD                                            370 / RUSSIAN FOLK-TALES. / CHAPTER I. / INTRODUCTORY.; lines 2152-2305",
    "start": "2152",
    "end": "2305",
    "translation": "Russian Fairy Tales: A Choice Collection of Muscovite Folk-lore",
    "notes": "Stable markdown line range generated from canonical text."
  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "The passage first presents a humorous tale about Marko the Rich. A poor peasant (moujik) gives a copeck to a beggar; Marko, seeming ashamed not to give, borrows a copeck from the moujik, promising to repay him. When the peasant repeatedly visits Marko's grand courtyard to reclaim the coin, Marko always claims to lack small change and postpones repayment, finally planning to feign death. He lies naked under the household holy pictures, covered by a cloth, while his wife weeps as if over a corpse and tells the peasant that Marko has just died. The peasant, asking to wash the body in return for his copeck, pours boiling water over the pretending Marko, then has him placed in a coffin and taken to the church, where he reads psalms over him. At night, robbers enter through a window to divide their stolen goods, quarrelling over a golden sabre. The peasant suggests the sabre should go to whoever cuts off the corpse's head; Marko suddenly springs up from the coffin, terrifying the robbers into fleeing and abandoning their loot. Marko then divides the money with the peasant but still insists he has no small change and never pays the copeck. The narrator next discusses the widely loved class of tales about simpletons (durak) in Russian Skazkas, explaining the different senses of the word and noting that in many fairy tales it applies to the youngest brother, the \"Boots\" of the family as named by Dr. Dasent, whose supposed foolishness is only others' misjudgment. The narrator adds that the durak in the following story is genuinely daft and comments on the conventional rhyming opening formula about a \"certain kingdom\". The tale \"The Fool and the Birch-Tree\" then begins: an old man dies leaving three sons, two clever and one a fool. By lot the clever brothers receive abundant goods, while the Simpleton gets only a very skinny ox. At fair-time, the clever brothers prepare to transact business; the Simpleton decides to go and sell his ox, leading it through a forest where a withered birch-tree creaks in the wind. He interprets the creaking as bargaining for his ox, sets the price at twenty roubles, leaves the ox tied to the birch on credit until the next day, and goes home to tell his brothers he has sold it.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A poor moujik feels pity for a beggar and gives him a copeck, after which Marko the Rich asks the moujik to lend him a copeck so he can also give something to the beggar, claiming he has no small change.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The moujik repeatedly visits Marko the Rich's spacious courtyard to ask for repayment of the copeck, but Marko always says he has no change and tells him to come again later.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "On one occasion Marko claims he has only a note for a hundred and, when the moujik cannot change it, postpones repayment for a fortnight.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Seeing the moujik approaching after the fortnight, Marko tells his wife he will strip naked, lie under the holy pictures, be covered with a cloth, and be mourned as a corpse so she can tell the moujik he died that morning.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Marko's wife follows his instructions, sits weeping bitterly beside him as if over a dead body when the moujik enters the room.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "When told that Marko has just died, the moujik says \"The kingdom of heaven be his!\" and offers, in return for his copeck, to perform a last service by washing the mortal remains.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The moujik takes a pot of boiling water and pours it over Marko's body while washing him, causing Marko to suffer and contort but try to hold out without revealing he is alive.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "After washing and laying out the body, the moujik tells Marko's wife to buy a coffin, have it taken into the church, and says he will go there to read psalms over the corpse.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Marko the Rich is placed in a coffin, taken into the church, and the moujik begins reading psalms over him as night falls.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "At night a window opens in the church and a band of robbers enters, then begins dividing their booty.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "Among the robbers' booty a golden sabre remains undistributed, and each robber tries to claim it so that none will yield.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "The moujik, hiding behind the altar, jumps out and proposes that the golden sabre belong to whoever cuts off the corpse's head.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Marko the Rich suddenly leaps up from the coffin \"like a madman,\" frightening the robbers so much that they abandon their spoil and flee.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "After the robbers flee, Marko suggests to the moujik that they divide the money, and they share it equally, each receiving a large portion.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "When the moujik again asks about his copeck, Marko says he still has no change, and the copeck is never repaid.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "The narrator introduces the next section as dealing with stories about simpletons, saying such tales are very popular with the public in all parts of the world.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "In the Skazkas a simpleton is called a durak, a term which can mean an idiot, a jester, or a \"ninny\" in village tales, and in fairy stories is often applied to the youngest of the \"Three Brothers,\" the \"Boots\" of the family as named by Dr. Dasent.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "The narrator explains that in tales about the heroic youngest brother his durachestvo or foolishness is subjective, arising from mistaken views held by family or neighbors, whereas the durak in the following tale is genuinely daft.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "The conventional Skazka opening \"In a certain tsarstvo, in a certain gosudarstvo\" is described as using two rhyming synonyms for \"kingdom\" or \"state.\"",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "In the story \"The Fool and the Birch-Tree\" an old man with three sons dies, and his property is divided among them by lot.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:21",
      "text": "Two of the sons are described as sharp-witted, while the third is explicitly called a fool and is termed the Simpleton.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:22",
      "text": "The clever brothers receive plenty of good things, but the Simpleton receives only one ox, described as very skinny.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:23",
      "text": "When fair-time arrives the clever brothers prepare to go to town to transact business, and the Simpleton decides to go too, to sell his ox.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:24",
      "text": "The Simpleton ties a cord to the horn of his ox and drives it through a forest.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:25",
      "text": "In the forest stands an old withered birch-tree which creaks whenever the wind blows.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:26",
      "text": "The Simpleton thinks the creaking birch is bargaining for his ox, sets the price at twenty roubles, and decides he will not take less.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:27",
      "text": "Hearing only creaking, the Simpleton imagines the birch is asking for credit, agrees to wait until the next day, ties the ox to the tree, and goes home.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:28",
      "text": "Later the clever brothers ask the Simpleton if he has sold his ox and for how much, and he answers that he has sold it for twenty roubles, though the money has not been shown.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    }
  ],
