{
  "record_id": "batch.motif.slavic-myths-folk-tales-curtin-gutenberg-l10517-l10631",
  "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/slavic/project-gutenberg/myths-folk-tales-russians-slavs-magyars-curtin.md",
  "passage_locator": {
    "label": "THE TABLE, THE PACK, AND THE BAG. / THE KING OF THE TOADS. / THE MOUSE-HOLE, AND THE UNDERGROUND KINGDOM. / THE CUIRASSIER AND THE HORNED PRINCESS.; lines 10517-10631",
    "start": "10517",
    "end": "10631",
    "translation": "Myths and Folk-tales of the Russians, Western Slavs, and Magyars",
    "notes": "Stable markdown line range generated from canonical text."
  },
  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "Twelve men from an unhappy regiment of cuirassiers desert and ride into mountainous forests. They use a high tree to check whether they are being pursued, and later to spot a distant light, which they ride toward after throwing a sword in its direction. In wild mountains they find an enormous open castle with a stable prepared for twelve horses and a hall laid with food for twelve people but no visible inhabitants. After eating, an old sorceress appears, welcomes them, and offers beds in a chamber containing twelve beds and twelve unlocked caskets. She strictly warns them that on pain of great punishment no one must look into the caskets.\nThe next morning everything is prepared for their comfort. The men praise the unexpected hospitality and discuss the caskets; some are strongly curious about the contents and doubt they can resist looking, while others warn against disobedience. They spend a pleasant day with several meals and go to bed. The following morning only three answer the chief’s call; the other nine are lifeless in their beds, and their nine horses are found dead in the stable. The survivors are terrified and resolve to leave this place of sudden death. Breakfast is now prepared only for three.\nThe sorceress returns and explains that \"sinful curiosity\" killed the nine men: they secretly rose at midnight, opened the caskets, looked inside, and died suddenly soon after lying down again. She says that if all had followed her advice, they could have lived happily in the castle for a whole year. Seeing that the survivors are too saddened to remain, she permits them to go wherever they wish but first allows each to open his own casket safely and take the contents as keepsakes that may be useful. Initially fearful, the three accept her repeated assurances and open their caskets. The first man receives a cap that makes the wearer invisible, the second a mantle that allows the wearer to fly through the air as high as he wishes, and the third a purse that produces ten ducats whenever it is shaken. They thank the sorceress for her hospitality and gifts, saddle their horses, and ride away \"with the Lord God.\"\nThey travel for a long time, talking about how they will enjoy their new gifts. Eventually they reach a large town, stay at an inn, and ask what is noteworthy there. The innkeeper tells them about a princess who loves playing cards, boasts that no one can play with her, defeats all challengers, and then has each man flogged out of the castle. The man with the magic purse decides to \"settle\" her play, goes to the castle, has himself announced, and declares his wish to play cards with the princess, while his two companions stay in the inn eating and drinking.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "A regiment of cuirassiers is encamped in a town and the soldiers have a very unpleasant life.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Twelve men from the regiment, including three sergeants and nine from the ranks, agree to desert and carry out their plan.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "One deserter suggests checking if they are pursued, and another dismounts, climbs a high tree, and reports that their pursuers will not overtake them.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "The deserters ride until dusk through a region where they see only mountains and forests.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:1"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "A man climbs a tree again to look for a light, sees one, and directs the others to ride where he throws his sword, toward the light.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The men arrive at a very large building in wild mountains, recognize it as an enormous open castle, and find a stable with oats ready for twelve horses and a hall with a table laid for twelve persons but no living soul present.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:2", "ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The deserters debate whether to eat the prepared food and drink; the chief decides they may, even if they must later pay for the entertainment.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "An old sorceress enters after they have eaten, greets them in \"our famous castle,\" and asks if the supper tasted well.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:3"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The sorceress offers them beds in the next chamber, which contains twelve beds and twelve unlocked caskets, and warns that no man must look at the caskets on pain of great punishment.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The sorceress bids them good night and departs through the opposite door; in the morning basins, towels, and food are prepared for each man.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:4", "ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "After breakfast, the men speak of the unexpectedly good cheer in the castle and then of the caskets, speculating about the splendid things inside.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Some of the men express strong curiosity and say they cannot refrain from looking into the caskets before evening, while others warn them not to do what they might regret.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:5"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "They spend a pleasant day in the castle with excellent meals and go to bed; the next morning the sun shines but most men are not stirring.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "When the chief calls the others, only two answer; the three find the remaining nine men lifeless in their beds and discover nine dead horses in the stable.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "One of the survivors says they must leave the place where their comrades have perished, for nothing can comfort them.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "Breakfast in the hall is prepared for only three men, who eat before the sorceress appears again.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:6", "ev:7"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "The sorceress tells them that \"sinful curiosity\" cost the nine men their lives; she says they rose at midnight, opened the caskets, looked at the contents, lay down again, and were overtaken by sudden death.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:7"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "The sorceress states that had all followed her advice, they might have enjoyed a pleasant time and lived joyously in the castle for a whole year.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:7"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "Seeing that the survivors are unwilling to stay, the sorceress says she will not keep them and invites each to look into his casket without fear or danger and take the contents as useful keepsakes.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:7", "ev:8"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "The three men are initially afraid to open the caskets but, after repeated assurances, they become bold and open them.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:8"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:21",
      "text": "The first man finds a cap with the power to make its wearer invisible to all others.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:8"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:22",
      "text": "The second man finds a mantle that allows its wearer to fly through the air as high as he wishes.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:8"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:23",
      "text": "The third man finds a purse that produces ten ducats whenever it is shaken.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:8"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:24",
      "text": "The sorceress bids the three men good-by; they thank her for hospitality and gifts, saddle their horses, and ride away from the castle \"with the Lord God.\"",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:8", "ev:9"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:25",
      "text": "As they travel, the three men talk about the good time they will have with their magical gifts.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:9"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:26",
      "text": "After a long journey, they arrive at a large town, lodge at an inn, and ask what is strange in the place.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:9", "ev:10"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:27",
      "text": "The innkeeper tells them about a princess who is extremely fond of playing cards, claims that no one can play with her, defeats every challenger, and then has him flogged out of the castle.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:10"]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:28",
      "text": "The man with the magic purse decides to \"settle\" the princess's play, goes to the castle, announces himself, and declares that he wishes to play with her, while the other two companions remain at the inn eating and drinking.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": ["ev:10"]
    }
