    "summary": "The passage first explains why gold is poetically called the needles or leaves of Glaser: in Asgard, before the doors of Valhal, stands a grove named Glaser whose leaves are red gold, said to be the fairest forest among gods and men. It then explains the kenning 'Sif's hair' for gold. Loki, Laufey's son, craftily cuts off all of Sif's hair. When Thor discovers this, he seizes Loki and threatens to break his bones unless Loki swears to have the swarthy elves make golden hair for Sif that will grow like natural hair. Loki goes to the dwarfs known as Ivald's sons, who forge the golden hair, the ship Skidbladner, and Odin's spear Gungner.\n\nLoki then wagers his head with the dwarf Brok that Brok's brother Sindre cannot make three treasures as good as these. In the smithy, Sindre successively places a pig-skin, gold, and iron in the furnace, each time ordering Brok to keep the bellows going. A fly repeatedly lands on Brok's hand, neck, and eyelids, stinging him; he perseveres until the last time, when blood blinds him and he briefly lets go. Sindre forges from these a boar with golden bristles, the ring Draupner, and a hammer whose production was nearly spoiled and which ends up with a short handle. He gives these to Brok and sends him with Loki to Asgard to settle the wager.\n\nIn Asgard, the gods sit on their doom-steads and agree that Odin, Thor, and Frey will judge. Loki gives Gungner to Odin, Sif's golden hair to Thor, and Skidbladner to Frey, explaining that the spear never misses, the hair will grow once placed on Sif's head, and the ship always has a fair wind and can be folded up and carried in a pocket. Brok presents his treasures: Draupner to Odin, which produces eight equal rings every ninth night; the boar to Frey, which runs through air and sea faster than any horse and whose shining bristles give light even in the worlds of darkness; and the hammer to Thor, which never takes damage, always returns to the thrower, can shrink to be hidden, but has a short handle. The gods decide the hammer is the best treasure and greatest protection against the frost-giants, and they rule that the dwarf has fairly won the wager.\n\nLoki offers to ransom his head, but Brok refuses. Loki attempts escape using shoes that let him run through air and over the sea, yet at the dwarf's request Thor catches him. When Brok wants to cut off Loki's head, Loki argues that only his head, not his neck, was pledged. Brok then tries to sew Loki's mouth shut, but his knife will not pierce Loki's lips. After he wishes for his brother's awl, it appears and allows him to pierce the lips; Brok sews Loki's mouth together with a thread and breaks the thread off. The thread used to sew Loki's mouth is called Vartare ('a strap').",
