    "summary": "Bulat the hero sharpens his sword and waits at the porch while Ivan Tsarevich’s dog is brought; the dog breaks free, runs to the porch, and Bulat cuts it in two, angering the Tsarevich, who keeps silent because of Bulat’s past service. The next day the Tsarevich’s favorite horse breaks its halter, gallops straight at him, and Bulat cuts off its head, increasing the Tsarevich’s anger. When the Tsarevich orders Bulat seized to be hanged, Vassilissa Kirbítyevna intercedes, saying that without Bulat the Tsarevich would never have won her. On the third day the favorite cow breaks away, runs at the Tsarevich, and Bulat beheads it as well, enraging the Tsarevich so much that he orders the headsman to execute Bulat immediately. Bulat asks to die by his own means, requesting permission to speak three speeches. As Bulat recounts the first night, when twelve doves flew to them in the open field and spoke, his body turns to stone up to the knees; after telling of the second night he becomes stone up to the girdle. Though the Tsarevich begs him not to finish, Bulat insists it is not worth living and, after telling of the third night, becomes wholly stone. The Tsarevich places the stone Bulat in a separate chamber and visits him daily with Vassilissa, weeping over him for years. One day, while weeping, the Tsarevich hears a voice from the stone Bulat asking why he weeps and saying that Bulat himself suffers in this state. The Tsarevich replies that he destroyed Bulat; the voice tells him he can save Bulat by killing his two children, a son and a daughter, collecting their blood in a vessel, and rubbing the stone with it. The Tsarevich tells Vassilissa; they mourn but decide to kill their children, gather the blood, and rub the stone. Bulat comes to life and asks whether they grieved for the children; they confirm that they did. Bulat then leads them to the children’s room, where the son and daughter are alive. Overjoyed, the parents hold a feast for all.",
