    "summary": "The narrator introduces the idea of \"magic waters\" and specifically a deadly \"Water of Death,\" contrasted with healing and strengthening waters. In the story that follows, \"The Blind Man and the Cripple,\" Baba Yaga is noted as a figure in one version and replaced by a Snake in a variant. The tale begins in a kingdom where a king and queen have a son, Prince Ivan, and appoint Katoma as his tutor. Before dying, the parents command Ivan to obey Katoma completely, promising prosperity if he does and death if he disobeys; Ivan follows this advice. When he reaches adulthood he wishes to marry and tells Katoma, who directs him to a great hall containing portraits of all the princesses in the world. Ivan is captivated by the portrait of Princess Anna the Fair, whose inscription states that she must marry any man whose riddle she cannot guess but may have the head cut off of any man whose riddle she does guess. Distressed, Ivan consults Katoma, who offers to accompany him and help. They travel for three years through many countries toward Anna’s land. On the road Katoma finds a purse full of money, takes the money, and uses the event to devise a riddle about \"Good\" lying on the road and being taken up with \"Good\" into their own \"Good,\" which he says the princess will not be able to solve with her magic-book. On arriving at Anna’s lofty palace, she sees them from her balcony, sends to ask their business, and then summons Ivan to present his riddle before her council of princes and boyars. She repeats her compact about riddles, Ivan poses the \"Good\" riddle, and Anna searches her magic-book but fails to discover the answer. Her council decides she must marry Ivan. Displeased, she prepares for the wedding but secretly plans to delay and destroy the bridegroom by overwhelming him with tasks. She calls Ivan, addresses him as her destined husband, and asks him to carry a lofty iron pillar from part of her kingdom into the palace kitchen and chop it into chunks for fuel. Ivan refuses, saying that he did not come to chop fuel and that such work should be done by his servant, Katoma dyadka of the oaken shapka.",
