    "summary": "Near the ruins of two pueblos in Chaco Canyon—Kintyél (Broad House) and Ki'ndotliz (Blue House)—stands a high cliff called Standing Rock (Tse`dezá`). The bead chant rite first became known to humans in this area. Two young men, one from each pueblo, go deer hunting and at sunset see a war-eagle soaring overhead, apparently descending onto Standing Rock. To mark the spot, they plant and align a forked stick as a sighting device, then return home. Their report leads the people to send four men who, using the stick, locate the eagle’s nest with two eaglets of different ages in a cleft on Standing Rock. After watching the nest, the communities meet in an estufa, decide which pueblo will claim which eaglet, and plan to reach the nest by lowering a man on a rope past an overhanging ledge. Since no one volunteers for the dangerous task, they choose a miserable Navaho beggar, Nahoditáhe (He Who Picks Up), who lives on scraps from sweepings and fireplace ashes. In the estufa they seat him and offer abundant food—paper bread, corn, and meat—promising such plenty for life if he agrees to be lowered in a basket and drop the eaglets to the bottom of the cliff. Reflecting that his life is already bitter and death inevitable, he consents. The next morning they feed him again, build a strong four-cornered carrying basket with strings tied to a long rope, gather a large party, and climb Standing Rock. At the summit, they attach the rope to the basket, instruct Nahoditáhe to drop the eaglets, and lower him until he reaches the level of the nest. As he is about to seize the eaglets, Wind whispers that the Pueblo people are not his friends and will not pull him up once he throws down the birds, advising him to get into the nest and stay. Nahoditáhe then has the basket swung against the cliff, clings to the rock, scrambles into the nest, and leaves the empty basket swinging.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "Kintyél (Broad House) and Ki'ndotliz (Blue House) are described as two pueblo houses in Chaco Canyon, now ruins but once inhabited by many people.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Near these pueblos is a high cliff called Tse`dezá` or Standing Rock.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "Near Kintyél, Ki'ndotliz, and Standing Rock, the bead chant rite was first practised, and the tale explains how it became known to humans.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "Two young men, one from Kintyél and one from Ki'ndotliz, go out to hunt deer but return weary and unsuccessful.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "At sunset the hunters see a war-eagle soaring overhead, watch it move away until it appears to descend onto the top of Standing Rock.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "To mark the location of the eagle, the hunters cut a forked stick, plant it in the ground, and align it so that looking over it from a certain crouching position will guide their sight to the spot.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "When the hunters report seeing the eagle, the story becomes the subject of much conversation and counsel, and the people decide to send four men the next morning to sight over the forked stick to find where the eagle lives.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Four designated men sight over the forked stick, conclude the eagle lives on the point of Standing Rock, climb to its summit, and observe the eagle and its young in a cleft below them all day.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "The scouts report seeing two young eagles of different ages; people from the two pueblos meet in an estufa and decide that Ki'ndotliz should have the elder eaglet and Kintyél the younger.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "The only way to reach the nest is to lower a man to it with a rope, past an overhanging ledge directly above the nest, making the descent dangerous; no one volunteers.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "A miserable Navaho beggar named Nahoditáhe lives near the pueblos, survives by searching sweepings and fireplace ashes for grains of corn or bits of paper bread, and is described as always hungry.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "The people conclude that they will induce Nahoditáhe to make the dangerous descent to the nest.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "In the estufa the people seat Nahoditáhe before a large basket of paper bread and bowls of boiled corn and meat, telling him to eat his fill, and he eats until he is satisfied.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "The people promise that Nahoditáhe will eat such abundance all his life and no longer have to scrape for grains of corn if he agrees to be put in a basket and lowered to the nest to catch the young eagles; after being asked four times he reflects on his hard, hungry life and inevitability of death and agrees.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "The next morning they give Nahoditáhe another good meal, make a strong carrying-basket with four corners and strings tied to each corner, and gather a large party to go to Standing Rock.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "At the top of Standing Rock they tie a long stout rope to the basket’s four strings, instruct Nahoditáhe to take the eaglets and drop them to the base of the cliff, place him in the basket, and lower him slowly until he reaches the level of the nest.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "As Nahoditáhe is about to grasp the eaglets, Wind whispers that the Pueblo people are not his friends, that they do not intend to feed him as promised, and that if he throws the young eagles down they will never pull him up again, advising him to get into the nest and stay there.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "Following Wind’s advice, Nahoditáhe calls for those above to swing the basket closer to the cliff, and when it touches he holds fast to the rock, scrambles into the nest, and leaves the empty basket swinging in the air.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    }
  ],
