    "summary": "Editorial and ethnographic notes describe the Navaho feather chant and bead chant as long healing ceremonies; explain devices like forked sticks used to locate night fires; identify plants, animals, and phenomena (tumbleweeds, frightened-weed, whirlwinds, arrow-snake, hawk); comment on beliefs about Spider Woman, color-directions, masked dancers, and great shells; and highlight recurring narrative motifs such as the wanderer who finds his former home intolerable and the meeting of messengers who exchange bows and quivers to validate their report, a scene appearing in more than one ceremony legend.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The feather chant (A-tsó-si-dze ha-tál or a-tsó-si ha-tál) is a nine-day ceremony using dry-paintings on the floor of the medicine-lodge, including animal representations, and formerly included a deer dance; it is now rarely celebrated and has only four priests living.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "The feather rite is good for many ailments but especially for \"deer disease,\" which may be contracted by sleeping on a dry, undressed or foul deer-skin or by a deer sneezing at or making marked demonstrations toward a person.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "The bead chant (Yó-i ha-tál or yói-dze ha-tál) is a nine-day ceremony that is becoming obsolete; there is said to be only one priest remaining, who learned it from his father but does not know as much about it as his father did.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A device of setting up forked sticks helps in locating fires seen by night and in remembering the position of distant objects, and this is often mentioned in Navaho tales.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "Equisetum hiemale and perhaps other species of Equisetum are identified as horse-tail plants referenced in the notes.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "Klis-ka', the arrow-snake, is described as a long slender snake that moves with great velocity, can fly for some distance through the air when racing off a cliff, is believed by Navahoes to be able to soar if it wished, has a red and blue belly and striped back, is six feet long or longer, and sometimes moves like a measuring-worm.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "Dr. H. C. Yarrow identifies the arrow-snake described in the legend as Bascanium flagelliforme.",
      "category": "other",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Accipiter cooperii is called gíni by the Navahoes.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Comparison with other passages suggests the Navahoes believe in more than one Spider Woman, possibly one for each world.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "Elsewhere in the text black is assigned to the east and white to the north in the Navaho color-direction system.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "The tumbleweed referred to is Amarantus albus, called tlotáhi nagi'si or rolling tlotáhi; tlotáhi is a name applied to several species of Amarantaceæ and allied Chenopodiacæ whose seeds formerly constituted an important part of the Navaho diet and are still eaten to some extent.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "Tsil-dil-gi'-si, meaning frightened-weed, scare-weed, or hiding-weed, is described as a yellow-flowered composite (Gutierrezia euthamiæ) whose dense foliage shelters snakes, lizards, and other animals when frightened, and which is used extensively in Navaho ceremonies for preparing and depositing sacrifices.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "Whirlwinds of no great violence are exceedingly common in the arid region and columns of whirling dust are often visible in extensive landscapes.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "In a full priestly myth of yói hatál the complete ceremonies, songs, and sacrifices taught to the Navaho would be given at the point referenced, but in the present account, told by an outsider, the ritual portion is omitted.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "In the myth of the Mountain Chant, a wanderer returning to his old home finds the odors of the place intolerable, and similar incidents occur in other Navaho myths.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "In the night chant (klédzi hatál), the first four masked characters who come out to dance in the public performance of the last night are called atsá`lei; this story suggests that similar characters belong to the bead chant (yói hatál).",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:13"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "Great shells associated with certain pueblos are mentioned in several legends; such shells may not be wholly mythical and, if transported from the coast to the Chaco Canyon before the introduction of the horse, would have been of great value among the Indians.",
      "category": "object",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "In the Mountain Chant myth, a courier going north to summon distant bands meets another messenger travelling from a distant camp to call people to a dance held in a circle of branches different from his own; the second messenger urges postponement so groups can attend each other's ceremonies, but the first refuses, saying his people wish to complete their dance, and the two exchange bows and quivers as a sign that they have met and that their reports will be true before parting; the same meeting of couriers is referenced in \"The Great Shell of Kintyél,\" and the editor notes that one legend is made to corroborate the other even though they belong to different rites.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:15"
      ]
    }
