    "summary": "A queen suffers a long sickness and cannot give birth; after six winters she feels near death and orders the child to be cut from her body. The operation is done, the boy kisses his mother before she dies, and he is named Volsung. Volsung grows large, strong, daring, becomes king over Hunland, and proves the greatest of warriors. When he reaches manhood, Hrimnir the giant sends him his daughter Ljod, previously known for bringing a fertility apple to Rerir, Volsung's father. Volsung marries Ljod; they live in love and good fortune, have ten sons and one daughter, with the eldest son Sigmund and the daughter Signy being twins and the foremost of Volsung's children. The Volsungs are described as a famous, high-minded, and mighty lineage. King Volsung then builds a noble hall around a great oak whose trunk stands inside and whose blossoming branches extend over the roof; the trunk is called Branstock.\nA powerful king, Siggeir of Gothland, comes to Volsung asking to marry Signy. Volsung and his sons approve, though Signy is reluctant but yields to her father's decision, and she is betrothed to Siggeir, who is to return to Volsung's hall for the wedding feast. At the feast, great fires burn along the hall with the tree standing midmost. In the evening an unknown, huge, one-eyed, ancient-seeming man enters, dressed in a spotted cloak, tight linen breeches, bare feet, and a slouched hat, carrying a sword. He walks to the Branstock and drives the sword deep into its trunk, declaring that whoever draws it shall receive it as a gift and will never bear a better sword. He then departs and no one knows who he is or where he went. Many noblemen and others attempt to pull out the sword but fail. Sigmund, Volsung's eldest son, then takes hold of the sword and draws it out easily, as if it were loose. All agree the weapon surpasses any they have seen. Siggeir offers Sigmund three times the sword's weight in gold to buy it; Sigmund refuses, saying Siggeir could have taken it himself if it had been his lot, and that Siggeir shall never have it whatever gold he offers. Siggeir grows angry at this answer yet, being wary and double-dealing, hides his anger and later plots revenge. A translator's note explains that the mysterious man is Odin, recognizable as one-eyed because he pledged his eye for a drink from Mimir's fountain, the source of all wisdom.\nLater Siggeir lies with Signy, and the next morning, with fair weather, he insists on leaving immediately lest wind and sea become impassable. Volsung and his sons do not hinder him, seeing he is eager to depart. Signy then tells her father she has no desire to go with Siggeir, that her heart does not favor him, and that by her foreknowledge and the fetch of their kin she knows great evil will come if the marriage is not quickly undone.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
