  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "The passage presents intertwined speeches and narrative involving Brynhild, Gunnar, Gudrun, Atli, Hogni, and various women of the hall. An unnamed man defends his loyalty to Gunnar despite a maiden's love. Gudrun mourns the death of a king so intensely that cups ring and geese cry, while Brynhild laughs once upon hearing her grief. Gunnar rebukes Brynhild, calling her hateful and fey, and wishes that she might see her brother Atli slain and bind his wounds. Brynhild responds that Atli fears no ill will and will outlive them, then recounts her youth in her brother's house, the arrival of the Giukings, Atli's threat to withhold her wealth unless she marries, and her inner debate between fighting in armor and choosing peace. She admits becoming more drawn to treasure, particularly the red rings of Sigmund's son, a great king seated amid gold on the horse Grani, whom she loved exclusively, and she vows that her woe will be avenged when she dies. Gunnar tries to embrace the queen and, together with others, attempts to change her mind, but she pushes them away, intending that no one hinder her \"long journey.\" Gunnar consults Hogni about stopping his wife's departure, but Hogni advises letting necessity rule, saying she will not be born again or return and was born for woe. Brynhild goes to the treasure hall, contemplates her wealth and the hungry bondmaids and maids, puts on a gold byrny, and stabs herself with a sword, then continues to give counsels as she dies. She offers gold, hangings, bed-gear, and fine clothes to any maid who wants them, but the women refuse to die, preferring to live and work. A young linen-clad damsel declares that no one should be forced to lose beloved life, and there is mention that she will burn on their bones, with fewer treasures at their departure, and that they will not come to see Brynhild with Menia's Meal. Brynhild then speaks to Gunnar of the ruin of his bride's life, warns his ship will not sail smoothly because of her death, predicts he will soon make peace with Gudrun, and foretells that Gudrun, as a wise woman, will fill a young wife with the hard memory of her dead husband. She prophesies the birth of a maiden, raised by her mother, whiter and brighter than the day, who will be named Swanhild and Sunbeam.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
