  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A king's son (Yanoshka) uses a golden rod to open an enchanted castle, resists the temptations of singing maidens, circles a world-sounding tree, and escapes as the door nearly traps him; the tree then appears in his home flowery garden, whose fame attracts visitors from seven worlds and draws the king's interest. An old witch, divining the king's intention, poisons his coffee, visits the garden, and tells the two children that the tree would be seven times more beautiful if a world-sweetly speaking bird sang upon it, saying the bird must be brought from an enchanted castle. The boy cannot rest until he seeks the bird, parts tearfully from his sister, and journeys across forty-nine kingdoms to enchanted castles guarded by devils. The eldest devil, a big lame one, gives him a golden rod and instructions to strike the castle wall three times, run through a glass corridor and nine chambers, ignore tempting sights and birds, and seize the sad world-sweetly speaking bird from a rusty cage. The boy follows the instructions, passes flowers, weapons, and singing golden-feathered birds, and finds the drooping, silent bird in the rusty cage among ornate golden and silver cages.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
  "literal_observations": [
