    "quote": "MYTHOLOGICAL.\n\nPrincipal Incarnations of Evil.\n\nOn the \"Mythical Skazkas\"--Male embodiments of Evil: 1. The Snake as the Stealer of Daylight; 2. Norka the Beast, Lord of the Lower World; 3. Koshchei the Deathless, The Stealer of Fair Princesses--his connexion with Punchkin and \"the Giant who had no Heart in his Body\"--Excursus on Bluebeard's Chamber; 4. The Water King or Subaqueous Demon--Female Embodiments of Evil: 1. The Baba Yaga or Hag, and 2. The Witch, feminine counterparts of the Snake.",
    "summary": "This passage is part of the table of contents for Ralston's \"Russian Fairy Tales.\" It outlines themes of Chapter I (Introductory), which covers the folk-tale and Skazka, their relation to Russian life, stories of courtship, death, burial, wailings for the dead, warnings against drink, jokes about women, tales of simpletons, a rhymed Skazka, and a legend. Chapter II (Mythological) includes a section on \"Principal Incarnations of Evil\" in mythical Skazkas. It lists male embodiments of evil—the Snake as the Stealer of Daylight, Norka the Beast as Lord of the Lower World, Koshchei the Deathless as the Stealer of Fair Princesses (with a noted connection to Punchkin and the Giant who had no Heart in his Body, and an excursus on Bluebeard's Chamber), and the Water King or Subaqueous Demon. Female embodiments of evil are named as Baba Yaga or Hag and the Witch, described as feminine counterparts of the Snake.",
