  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "In a framed storytelling scene, the attendant Waíhusiwa addresses his listeners and recounts an old tale. Under Thunder Mountain, in the village K’iákime, a beautiful maiden, daughter of the priest-chief, obsessively bathes and washes at a spring sacred to the Sea Serpent Kólowissi, thereby defiling its waters. Angered, Kólowissi, a god who can take any form, appears in the spring as a beautiful child. The maiden, believing the child abandoned, carries it up to her isolated upper room and becomes lovingly absorbed in playing with it, ignoring mealtime calls from her family. The wary father suspects something uncanny because no true mother would leave a child in a spring. That night, after the maiden and child lie down, the child’s feigned sleep turns into a transformation: it elongates into an enormous serpent that fills the room with gleaming coils, encircles the sleeping maiden, and takes its own tail into its mouth as the night passes.",
    "language": "English",
