  "literal_observations": [
    {
      "id": "obs:1",
      "text": "The tale is introduced with a note that the priest of the K’iáklu epic-ritual is not allowed to initiate short folk-stories; this story is told instead by an attendant Indian named Waíhusiwa.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:2",
      "text": "Waíhusiwa begins the telling with the exclamation \"Son ah tehi!\" and listeners reply \"É-so\" or \"Tea-tu,\" glossed as \"Certainly\" or \"Be it well.\"",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:3",
      "text": "In the times of the forefathers, there was a village called K’iákime (“Home of the Eagles”) located under Thunder Mountain, now described as ruined with missing roofs, decayed ladders, and cold hearths.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:4",
      "text": "A maiden, described as beautiful, lived in K’iákime and is identified as the daughter of the priest-chief.",
      "category": "relationship",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:5",
      "text": "At the foot of the village terrace is a sacred spring of water, later called the Pool of the Apaches, which was then sacred to Kólowissi, the Serpent of the Sea.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:6",
      "text": "The maiden has a strong aversion to any dirt or dust on her person or clothing and spends most of her time washing her belongings and bathing herself in the waters of the sacred spring.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:7",
      "text": "The text states that the sacred waters of the Serpent of the Sea should not have been defiled by such washing and bathing.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:3"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:8",
      "text": "Kólowissi, identified as the Serpent of the Sea, becomes troubled and angry at the sacrilege in his spring and speaks about the maiden defiling the sacred waters.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:9",
      "text": "Kólowissi devises a plan to prevent further sacrilege and to punish the maiden who is its author.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:4"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:10",
      "text": "When the maiden next comes to the spring, she sees a beautiful little child seated amidst the waters, splashing them, smiling, and cooing.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:11",
      "text": "The narrative explains that the child is actually the Sea Serpent wearing the semblance of a child and notes that a god may assume any form at its pleasure.",
      "category": "attribute",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:12",
      "text": "The maiden looks north, south, east, and west but sees no one and no traces of people who might have brought the child to the spring.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:13",
      "text": "The maiden reasons that the child must belong to some cruel mother who has deserted it in the spring and resolves to take the child in her arms and care for it.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:14",
      "text": "The maiden speaks softly to the child, takes it in her arms, hastens up the hill to her house, climbs the ladder, and carries the child into the room where she sleeps.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:6"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:15",
      "text": "Because of her dislike of dirt and dust, the maiden dwells apart from the rest of her family in a room by herself above all other apartments.",
      "category": "setting",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:16",
      "text": "In her private room, the maiden sits on the floor and plays with the child, laughing and smiling at him, while he responds with cooings and smiles; her heart becomes very happy and loving, and she forgets the lapse of time.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:17",
      "text": "Meanwhile the younger sisters have prepared a meal and wait for the elder sister’s return.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:18",
      "text": "The old father assumes the maiden is at the spring washing and bathing, and sends a younger sister to call her.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:19",
      "text": "The younger sister finds no trace of the maiden at the spring, climbs the ladder to the private room, and sees the maiden playing with the child.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:20",
      "text": "After hearing about the child, the father sits silent and thoughtful, aware that the spring’s waters are sacred.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:8",
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:21",
      "text": "When the rest of the family excitedly runs to see the child, the father orders them to come back, arguing that no mother would leave her child in any spring and stating that there is more meaning in the situation than appears.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:22",
      "text": "When the family calls the maiden to come down to the prepared meal, she cannot be induced to leave the child.",
      "category": "action",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:23",
      "text": "The father remarks that a woman will not leave a child for any inducement, and even less her own child.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:24",
      "text": "The child grows sleepy, the maiden places it on a bed, and, becoming sleepy herself, lies down beside it and falls asleep.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:25",
      "text": "The narrative states that the maiden’s sleep is genuine, but the child’s sleep is feigned.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:26",
      "text": "While the maiden sleeps, the child becomes elongated and transforms into an enormous serpent that coils round and round the room until it is full of scaly, gleaming circles.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:27",
      "text": "The great serpent places its head near the maiden’s head, surrounds her with its coils, and finally takes its own tail into its mouth.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:11"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:28",
      "text": "The night passes; in the morning the breakfast is prepared, the maiden still does not descend, and her younger sisters become impatient.",
      "category": "sequence",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "obs:29",
      "text": "The father states that now that the maiden has the child to play with, she will care little for anything else and that a child is enough to occupy a woman’s entire attention.",
      "category": "speech",
      "evidence_refs": [
        "ev:12"
      ]
    }
