      "type": "summary",
      "locator": "lines 2995-3101",
      "quote_or_summary": "At the foot of the terrace stood a sacred spring of water, later known as the Pool of the Apaches, which was sacred to Kólowissi, the Serpent of the Sea. The maiden, having an extreme passion for neatness and cleanliness, cannot tolerate any dirt and spends much time washing her belongings and bathing herself in this spring, thereby defiling the sacred waters.",
      "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/zuni/project-gutenberg/zuni-folk-tales-cushing.md",
      "rights_note": "Public domain; Project Gutenberg edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "ev:4",
      "type": "summary",
      "locator": "lines 2995-3101",
      "quote_or_summary": "The narrative stresses that the waters sacred to the Serpent of the Sea should not have been defiled. Kólowissi becomes troubled and angry at the sacrilege, asks why the maiden defiles his spring with dirt from her apparel and person, and resolves to prevent the sacrilege and punish its author by devising a plan.",
      "source_text_path": "texts/public-domain/zuni/project-gutenberg/zuni-folk-tales-cushing.md",
      "rights_note": "Public domain; Project Gutenberg edition."
    },
    {
      "id": "ev:5",
      "type": "summary",
      "locator": "lines 2995-3101",
      "quote_or_summary": "When the maiden next visits the spring, she sees a beautiful little child sitting amidst the waters, splashing, cooing, smiling, and playing. The text explains that this child is actually the Sea Serpent in the semblance of a child and notes that a god may assume any form at its pleasure. The maiden looks in all four cardinal directions but sees no one.",
