  "canonical_text": {
    "quote": "",
    "summary": "A narrator addresses young listeners, lamenting that youths of recent generations lack the sturdiness of the ancients and urging them to draw a lesson from the story. He introduces a poor, ill-favored outcast young man of Moki, socially counted last among the men but destined to be the hero of the tale. In Walpi, on the first mesa of the Mokis, lives an exceptionally beautiful and well-adorned maiden, much desired by the youths of the Moki towns. She rejects all suitors, declaring that only a hero will win her, and proposes that any man who would have her must go to the enemy nation of Zuñi, far to the east over the mountains, slay its men, and bring back many scalps as proofs of valor and as presentations to the warrior societies; such a man she will admire, cherish, and marry. The handsome suitors shrink from the dangerous demand and depart crestfallen, loving her less than they fear the valiant Zuñi warriors. The outcast youth, who lives among captive dogs, eagles, and other creatures and eats discarded scraps, overhears the jilted lovers discussing the maiden’s terms and rejecting them. He resolves, presumptuously, to woo her himself. One night he goes to her father’s house, calls at the rooftop entrance in a refined and affable tone, and the family, expecting a splendid suitor, invite him in, only to be surprised by the ragged vagabond who descends the ladder. The old father nonetheless greets him politely, seats him by the fireplace, has food set before him, which the youth eats sparingly, and includes him in the evening smoking and conversation. When asked his purpose, the youth explains that he has heard of the maiden’s conditions and wishes to try for her despite his limited merits and great love. The maiden, whose opinion of him softens upon hearing his voice, is amused yet chooses to test his seriousness by making the terms harder than for the other suitors: he must single-handedly slay some of the valiant Zuñi warriors and bring home a goodly number of their scalps for the joy of their warriors and people, and then she will indeed wed him. The youth accepts these terms, bids the household a happy night, and goes out into the dark.",
    "language": "English",
    "quote_policy": "summarized"
  },
