    "summary": "In the very ancient past at the Home of the Eagles under Thunder Mountain, the secluded daughter of a great priest-chief has never left her house and is unseen by the townspeople, known only to her parents. Each day at noon the Sun looks through a small roof window, descends on a luminous trail of his rays, speaks with her, and is described as her lover and sole companion besides her parents. The curious townspeople, led by the chief of the dances, organize a Plume dance with macaw feather plumes in the plaza before her house hoping she will appear, but she remains inside, weaving trays in a back room, and no one sees her. The Sun, not wishing her to be seen by other men, places a wise Eagle in a cage on the roof to guard her; she feeds and waters the bird. After repeated dances fail, the two Warrior Priests of the Bow become angry and order the warrior festival Óinahe dance, declaring that she must come forth or die. They call the people to prepare for the dance, and the townsfolk anticipate finally seeing the beautiful girl. The two warriors then climb Thunder Mountain to the summit dwelling of the war gods Áhaiyúta and Mátsailéma and their grandmother. Explaining their duty to choose dances that keep the people happy and their desire to make the priest-chief’s daughter appear or else die, they ask the gods for advice. The war gods agree that the warriors\u0019 wish shall be fulfilled and decree that the girl must die if she does not come forth at the bidding of the Óinahe dance.",
